<section class="page-head"><div class="container"><span class="status-pill completed">Completed</span><div class="eyebrow">NLP · Transformers · Public Health</div><h1>From COVID-19 Vaccine Hesitancy to Mpox</h1><p class="section-lead">A Comparative Study of Stance Labelling and Machine-Learning Classification across two published collaborative public-health NLP studies.</p></div></section>
<section><div class="container content">
<section class="story-section"><h2>Project at a glance</h2><div class="glance-study"><h3>COVID-19 study</h3><div class="story-grid"><div class="story-step"><strong>1 · Objective</strong><p>Characterise public attitudes towards COVID-19 vaccination in South Africa using supervised text classification.</p></div><div class="story-step"><strong>2 · Data</strong><p>Approximately 30,000 manually labelled South African vaccine-related tweets with positive, neutral and negative labels.</p></div><div class="story-step"><strong>3 · Method</strong><p>Compare classical, recurrent and transformer models, including BERT and RoBERTa, using manually labelled examples.</p></div><div class="story-step"><strong>4 · Evaluation</strong><p>Compare models using F1-score and analyse the effect of the difficult neutral class on three-class performance.</p></div><div class="story-step story-impact"><strong>5 · Outcome</strong><p>RoBERTa produced the strongest three-class result at about 61% F1, rising to roughly 80% F1 when the neutral class was removed.</p></div></div></div><div class="glance-study"><h3>Mpox study</h3><div class="story-grid"><div class="story-step"><strong>1 · Objective</strong><p>Test whether models adapted to COVID-19 health discourse transfer to Mpox stance classification and how much Mpox-specific supervision improves them.</p></div><div class="story-step"><strong>2 · Data</strong><p>20,604 manually labelled Mpox-related tweets collected during the 2022 outbreak.</p></div><div class="story-step"><strong>3 · Method</strong><p>Compare lexicon-based methods and transformer models, including COVID-adapted BERT/RoBERTa before and after Mpox-specific fine-tuning.</p></div><div class="story-step"><strong>4 · Evaluation</strong><p>Measure F1 and accuracy against human labels, including comparison with VADER and TextBlob.</p></div><div class="story-step story-impact"><strong>5 · Outcome</strong><p>After Mpox-specific fine-tuning, BERT reached 68% F1 and RoBERTa 69% F1, outperforming the simpler lexicon-based methods.</p></div></div></div></section>
<h2>Context and objective</h2>
<p>The synthesis examines how reliably context-sensitive human labelling can support stance classification in noisy public-health social-media data, and how well transformer models adapted to COVID-19 health discourse transfer to Mpox before and after Mpox-specific fine-tuning.</p>

<h2>Data and constraints</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>30,000</strong> manually labelled South African vaccine-related tweets in the COVID-19 study.</li>
<li><strong>20,604</strong> manually labelled tweets from the 2022 Mpox outbreak.</li>
<li>The COVID-19 annotation process used <strong>11 annotators</strong> and iterative consensus refinement.</li>
<li>Mean Cohen's kappa improved from <strong>0.67 to 0.85</strong> as annotation rules were reviewed and clarified.</li>
<li>The framework explicitly considered slang, sarcasm, punctuation, emojis and tone.</li>
</ul>
<p>The two corpora are related methodologically but are not treated as matched datasets: they differ in geographic scope, collection period, search criteria, public-health context and class distribution.</p>

<h2>Technical solution</h2>
<p>The COVID-19 study compared SVM, LSTM, Bidirectional LSTM, BERT-base-cased and RoBERTa-base. The Mpox study evaluated NLP-Town BERT, Cardiff-NLP RoBERTa, COVID-19 BERT and COVID-19 RoBERTa before and after Mpox-specific fine-tuning. VADER and TextBlob were also evaluated against human labels.</p>

<h2>Evaluation and evidence</h2>
<p>Human annotation is treated as part of the modelling system rather than a background step. Model comparisons use F1 and accuracy where reported, while cross-crisis transfer is evaluated before and after Mpox-specific fine-tuning.</p>

<h2>Key results</h2>
<ul>
<li>COVID-19 three-class classification: strongest overall F1 was <strong>60% for BERT</strong> and <strong>61% for RoBERTa</strong>.</li>
<li>Removing the neutral class increased both transformer models to approximately <strong>80% F1</strong>.</li>
<li>For Mpox, COVID-19 BERT and COVID-19 RoBERTa each achieved <strong>45% F1 / 47% accuracy</strong> before Mpox-specific fine-tuning.</li>
<li>After fine-tuning on labelled Mpox data, performance increased to <strong>68% F1 for BERT</strong> and <strong>69% F1 for RoBERTa</strong>.</li>
<li>VADER achieved <strong>47% F1 / 50% accuracy</strong>; TextBlob achieved <strong>37% F1 / 40% accuracy</strong>.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Limitations and scope</h2>
<p>This repository is a <strong>synthesis of published results</strong>, not a new modelling experiment. It introduces no new model training or unreported performance claims. The two studies also differ in context and data collection, so their scores are not treated as a controlled head-to-head benchmark.</p>

<h2>Practical relevance</h2>
<p>The combined evidence suggests that prior training on related health discourse can provide a useful starting representation, but does not remove the need for new domain-specific supervision. The work illustrates how large volumes of public-health opinion can be analysed while retaining human-labelled context.</p>

<h2>What this case proves</h2>
<p>I can work across human annotation, classical ML, recurrent neural networks and transformers; evaluate cross-domain transfer; and interpret public-health NLP results without reducing nuanced stance to simplistic sentiment.</p>

<h2>Evidence</h2>
